{ Original Hippopotamus as White course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. g3 d5 2. Bg2 e5 3. d3 Nc6 4. e3 Nf6 5. b3 Bd6 6. Bb2 O-O 7. Nd2 Re8 8. Ne2 Be6 9. a3 Qd7 10. h3 Rad8 { Laboratory starts here. White reaches the familiar two-rank formation while Black occupies the center. This quiet illustrative route is not a claim that all ten setup moves are best in every position. Resolve king safety and choose c4, d4 or e4 as a central challenge. Identify which bishop will become active after the chosen break. } 11. Nf3 { Candidate comparison. Develops the king knight toward e5 and d4. In the King's Gambit it also controls h4, making an immediate queen check there unattractive. } 11... h6 12. b4 a6 13. d4 e4 14. Ne5 Qc8 15. Nxc6 { Candidate comparison. The knight captures a knight on c6. Calculate the best reply before assuming a recapture, then recount material and opened lines. } 15... bxc6 16. Nf4 Bf5 17. Bf1 Nh7 18. h4 Nf6 19. Be2 { Candidate comparison. Places the bishop on e2. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 19... g6 20. Rc1 Bg4 *

