{ Original Hippopotamus as White course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. g3 h5 2. Nf3 h4 3. Nxh4 e5 4. e4 Nc6 5. d3 d5 6. exd5 Qxd5 7. Bg2 Qe6 8. a3 Bd7 9. O-O O-O-O 10. Re1 Nge7 { Laboratory starts here. Black attacks the g3 pawn before building a center. Nf3 develops and can capture on h4 when Black advances without support. Return the knight to a useful square if attacked and complete development. Accepting a pawn is useful only if it does not cost excessive time or king safety. } 11. Nf3 { Candidate comparison. Develops the king knight toward e5 and d4. In the King's Gambit it also controls h4, making an immediate queen check there unattractive. } 11... Qf5 12. h4 Qh5 13. Nbd2 f6 14. Nf1 Nf5 15. c3 { Candidate comparison. Places the pawn on c3. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 15... Kb8 16. b4 Nd6 17. a4 Bc8 18. Ba3 Qh7 19. b5 { Candidate comparison. Places the pawn on b5, attacking knight on c6. A legal capture may still be unsound if the target is defended. } 19... Na5 20. Qe2 Nf7 *

