{ Original Hippopotamus as White course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. g3 d5 2. Bg2 e5 3. d3 Nf6 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. O-O Be7 6. c4 O-O 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. Nc3 Be6 9. Bd2 Qd7 10. a3 a5 { Laboratory starts here. White challenges d5 with active development and a castled king. This useful departure resembles a Reti or reversed King's Indian structure more than a complete Hippo. Compare ...d4, ...dxc4 and maintaining tension. Develop the queen side according to the resulting pawn structure. } 11. Rc1 { Candidate comparison. Places the rook on c1. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 11... Nxc3 12. Bxc3 f6 13. Qc2 a4 14. e3 Bd5 15. Nh4 { Candidate comparison. Places the knight on h4. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 15... Bb3 16. Qe2 Rad8 17. Be4 Bc5 18. Bxe5 Bxe3 19. Bxc6 { Candidate comparison. The bishop captures a knight on c6. Calculate the best reply before assuming a recapture, then recount material and opened lines. } 19... bxc6 20. Qxe3 fxe5 *

