{ Original Hippopotamus as White course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. g3 d5 2. Bg2 Nf6 3. d3 e6 4. Nf3 Be7 5. O-O O-O 6. Nbd2 c5 7. e4 Nc6 8. Re1 Qc7 9. c3 h6 10. Qe2 Rd8 { Laboratory starts here. White adopts a King's Indian Attack center because Black's structure makes it useful. The b-pawn remains flexible instead of being committed automatically. Support e4, then assess e5 and the available knight routes. Use the KIA course for deeper study of this exact center. } 11. a3 { Candidate comparison. Controls nearby wing squares and may restrain a piece or pawn. Compare this useful purpose with the time lost to central development. } 11... b5 12. exd5 Nxd5 13. a4 b4 14. c4 Nf6 15. Nb3 { Candidate comparison. Places the knight on b3. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 15... Bb7 16. Bf4 Qc8 17. Rad1 Re8 18. h4 Bf8 19. Be3 { Candidate comparison. Places the bishop on e3. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 19... Nd7 20. Nfd2 Qc7 *

